--- abstract

Participation in the IETF is by individuals.  This document makes that
principle concrete for an environment in which software agents can
produce and submit text.  Each Datatracker account belongs to one
human who has acknowledged the Note Well.  Every IETF Contribution is
vouched for by one human who is accountable for it.  Anonymous
Contributions are not accepted, and no human may use more than one
identity in the same piece of work.

--- middle

# Introduction

The IETF has always said that its participants act as individuals.  The
existing statements of this principle are collected in
{{existing-principle}}.

Those statements were written when every message to a mailing list and
every Internet-Draft was typed by a person.  That is no longer a safe
assumption.  Agents can now draft text, post to mailing lists, open
issues, and submit documents, and can do so at a volume no person
could.

This document doesn't restrict the tools people use.  It states who is
responsible for what those tools produce.

# Conventions and Definitions

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

This document uses "Contribution", "Contributor", and "Participant" as
defined in {{RFC8179}} and {{RFC5378}}.

Human:
: A natural person.

Account:
: A Datatracker account, and the credentials associated with it.

Agent:
: Software that produces or submits text, or takes other actions,
  on behalf of a human.

# Requirements

## One Account per Human

Each human participating in the IETF SHOULD have one Datatracker
account.  {{multiple-identities}} describes the limited case in which a
human holds more than one.

A human MAY associate multiple email addresses with their account.
Using different addresses for different affiliations, or for personal
and work use, is common and expected.  Addresses on one account are
linked, and the IETF treats them as one participant.

## One Human per Account {#one-human-per-account}

Each Datatracker account MUST belong to exactly one human.  That human
MUST have acknowledged the Note Well {{NOTEWELL}}.

An account MUST NOT be shared between people, and MUST NOT be operated
by an organization or a role in place of a named human.

## Every Contribution Has an Accountable Human

Every Contribution MUST be vouched for by one human, who is accountable
for it as its Contributor.

That human MAY use any tools, including agents, to prepare or submit
the Contribution.  The obligations that apply to a Contributor, including
IPR disclosure under {{RFC8179}} and conduct under {{RFC7154}}, apply to
the human who vouches for it.  They apply in the same way whether the
human typed the text or an agent produced it.

# Anonymity {#anonymity}

This document uses "anonymous" and "pseudonym" as defined in
{{Section 3.3 of RFC6973}}.

Anonymity has real value.  People use it to take part in political
life where speaking under their own name would put them, their
families, or their jobs at risk.  {{RFC8280}} treats anonymity as a
human rights consideration in protocol design for these reasons.

Anonymous participation is still incompatible with the IETF standards
process.  The process depends on knowing which human made each
Contribution:

* A Contributor grants rights in their Contribution and makes
  representations about it ({{Section 5 of RFC5378}}).  A grant or a
  representation from nobody in particular is worth nothing.

* IPR disclosure obligations attach to IPR that the Contributor
  "reasonably and personally" knows of, including IPR held by their
  employer or sponsor ({{Section 5.6 of RFC8179}}).  Nobody can tell
  whether that obligation has been met without knowing who the
  Contributor is.

* Rough consensus is judged by chairs who read the discussion.  If one
  human can appear as several participants, the chairs can't tell how
  much support or opposition there really is.

* The guidelines for conduct {{RFC7154}} and the moderation processes
  {{RFC9945}} act on people.  A sanction that can be escaped by
  creating a new account has no effect.

* Affiliations "are often used for identification" ({{Section 3.2 of
  RFC9281}}).  Readers weigh an argument partly by knowing who is
  making it and what interests they may have.

Every Contribution MUST therefore be attributable to an identified
human, as required by {{one-human-per-account}}.  The IETF does not
accept anonymous Contributions.

# Multiple Identities {#multiple-identities}

A human may want to take part in the IETF under an identity that is not
publicly linked to their existing account.  Reasons include changing
employers, working in an area that is sensitive for their employer or
government, or separating personal and professional work.

A human MAY hold more than one account, each used for a different
piece of work, without publicly linking them.  Each account is still
subject to every requirement in this document: it belongs to one
identified human who has acknowledged the Note Well, and that human is
accountable for every Contribution made from it.

A human MUST NOT use more than one identity to contribute to the same
piece of work.  For the purposes of this document, a "piece of work"
includes at least:

* an Internet-Draft and any later document derived from it;

* any discussion, issue, review, or consensus call about such a
  document; and

* any nomination, petition, or other process in which participants
  are counted.

A human MUST NOT create a new account to avoid a sanction applied to
an existing one, or to avoid the history attached to it.

# Open Questions

This document is a starting point for discussion.  These questions are
expected to be debated.

* Is a working group the right scope for a "piece of work", or is a
  single document enough?  Working group scope is easier to check, but
  it may exclude people who have good reasons to keep two lines of
  work apart.

* Should an account with no public link to a human's other accounts
  still be linked privately, for example by disclosure to the IETF
  Secretariat or the Ombudsteam?  That would allow enforcement of
  {{multiple-identities}} without exposing the link publicly.

* Is a pseudonym acceptable on a single account, if the human behind it
  is known to the IETF and accountable in the same way as anyone else?

* How should the IETF verify that an account belongs to one human,
  and how much verification is proportionate?

# Existing Principle {#existing-principle}

The following documents state that participation in the IETF is by
individuals.

{{Section 3.2 of RFC9281}} (BCP 11):

> For all purposes relevant to the Internet Standards development
> process, membership in the IETF and its WGs is defined to be
> established solely and entirely by individuals who participate in
> IETF and WG activities.  These individuals do not formally represent
> any organizations they may be affiliated with, although affiliations
> are often used for identification.

{{Section 1 of RFC2418}} (BCP 25):

> There is no formal membership in the IETF.  Participation is open to
> all. \[...\] Participation is by individual technical contributors,
> rather than by formal representatives of organizations.

{{Section 5 of RFC8179}} (BCP 79):

> This document refers to the IETF Participant making disclosures,
> consistent with the general IETF philosophy that Participants in the
> IETF act as individuals.

{{Section 1 of RFC8179}} defines a "Contributor" as "an individual
submitting a Contribution", and a "Participant" as "any individual
Participating in an IETF discussion or activity".  {{Section 1 of
RFC5378}} (BCP 78) uses the same definition of "Contributor".

{{Section 2.2 of RFC7241}}:

> All IETF participants act as individuals.  There is no concept of
> "IETF membership".

{{Section 4.1 of RFC9680}}:

> While IETF participants are expected to participate as individuals,
> their actions could still be construed as representing their
> employer, whatever their role.

{{Section 2.1 of RFC8718}}:

> Meeting attendees participate as individuals.

{{Appendix A.1 of RFC6702}} describes the IPR obligations as "personal
requirements applying to all IETF participants as individuals".

{{Section 5 of RFC2026}} notes that the IAB and IESG "are composed of
individuals who may participate, as individuals, in the technical work
of the IETF".

The final version of the Tao of the IETF, archived in {{RFC9592}}, says
"There is no membership in the IETF."

The IRTF's code of conduct {{RFC9775}} says a generative tool must not
be listed as an author of a document, and that significant amounts of
AI-generated content must be disclosed.

